00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
after a violent mob attacked the u.s  capitol on january the 6th the biden  administration  made so-called domestic terrorism a top  priority  resources in fbi field offices have been  moved more federal lawyers employed to  prosecute cases  attorney general merrick garland says  it's the biggest threat facing america  in the fbi's view the top domestic  violent extremist threat comes from  racially or ethnically motivated violent  extremists  specifically those who advocated for the  superiority of the white race  in a statement president joe biden said  domestic terrorism caused by hatred  prejudice and other extremism is a stain  on the american soul  it goes against everything our country  strives for  garland was the federal prosecutor in  the oklahoma bombing  the biggest case of domestic terrorism  in u.s history  168 people died that was in 1995.  in recent years the number of attacks  has increased from the targeting of  members of congress by a left-wing  gunman at a baseball game  the shooting at the pulse nightclub in  orlando where 49 people died  and the car attack on anti-fascist  protesters in charlottesville and  virginia which killed a young woman  now the fbi plans to increase  intelligence sharing work with tech  companies to eliminate content that  could incite extremism  and work across government to identify  and root out  potential attackers or supporters even  in military and law enforcement  the national strategy is designed to  coordinate and provide a principled path  for the federal government's efforts to  counter  the heightened domestic terrorism threat  using all available tools  the trump white house was often  reluctant even to acknowledge the issue  of domestic terrorism  often comparing the threat from  right-wing extremists  to that of black lives matter protests  and antifa the anti-fascist group  even though intelligence reports said  that white supremacy was a key driver  of domestic terrorism this plan  acknowledges the elevated threat  and asks congress to find 100 million  dollars  to help fight it the justice department  led by garland is evaluating whether to  ask congress to pass  a specific law addressing what it  regards as domestic terrorism  they currently don't exist many  lawmakers believe  existing laws are enough alan fisher al  jazeera  the white house
 

